YAFAP

Well, it finally happened!  In a recent desire to finish a long-ago 
begun quest, I downloaded Nethack onto my computer.  I started playing 
10 years ago without any spoilers and running around the dungeon as an 
elf with the Palantir of Westernesse and the Amulet of Yendor and the 
Wizard chasing me all the while.  I had not realized that the amulet 
that had been in hand was actually a "you-know-what".  Over the last 
few months I pored over the spoilers and lurked around on RGRN 
learning all that I could.  Even still it took a few months.  So, now 
with much joy I post my YAFAP.  Truthfully, I had intended this to be 
my first AND last ascension post, but I've since ascended twice more.  
I think I'll try and ascend with one of each class and maybe even add 
in some conducts.

First of all, much thanks to the folks here on RGRN for all of the 
advice.  Thanks also to the makers and upkeepers of the spoilers and 
patches.

The biggest lesson learned over the course of this first ascension was 
adaptation.  The patterns and play style that achieve goals at one 
stage in the game are not necessarily appropriate or best for other 
times in the game.  Pets are a good example: awesome in the beginning 
of the game but not necessary in the end-game.  The trick is knowing 
when.

I decided to play an archeologist so as to enjoy the finer points of 
spell casting as well as melee and I actually grew quite happy with 
the archeologist's starting equipment.  Starting out with a sack, a 
tinning kit, a pick-axe, and a touchstone make for some conveniences.  
The sack makes credit cloning much less tedious; the tinning kit makes 
acquiring resistances and 18/** strength straightforward; the pick-axe 
gains some gold from vaults, and the touchstone makes for a cleaner 
inventory.

The Minetown Protection Run ( TM)
I have taken to attempting the MPR and intending to stay at LVL 1 and 
buy protection at a low rate.  The first stop was Sokoban for food 
(mainly) and wands with which to engrave Elbereth.  All went well but 
was unable to get to the Bag of Holding due to the giant mimics.  
There were two unremarkable shops in the upper levels of the dungeon 
but overall it went well and with 10,000 zorkmids, I bought myself to -
1 AC.

Big Room:
I was nervous about the Big Room as I've lost many promising 
characters to the hordes therein.  This time I was prepared with a 
wand of digging and a ring of conflict!  Slip the ring on, engrave 
Elbereth, and voila! ... carnage!  I had to melee with a few elves but I 
was never in danger.

The Search for Reflection:
Once I started to see elves and other @'s running around the dungeon I 
became very nervous about wand-wielders.  I've lost promising 
characters to random wands of death before.  This paranoia probably 
made the difference in this game between ascending and YAAD as I did 
find an Ogre king with a wand of death on level 17.  I avoided any 
contact with @'s, instead doing all possible to put my pets upon them 
rather than engage any in combat.  I reasoned that there were 3 ways 
for me to acquire reflection since Sokoban had the bag: 1) sit on a 
throne, 2) wand of wishing in castle, 3) Perseus' statue.  The throne 
yielded no wish but it did result in the genocide of green slimes.  To 
this date I had never seen a green slime before but had heard that 
they were dangerous.  The Castle seemed too dangerous so I levitated 
over to Medusa and used a mirror on her.  The RNG smiled upon me for 
Perseus indeed had a shield of reflection.  (but he did not have a 
blindfold, which, apparently was his downfall)  Whew! That was a 
relief.

Polypiling:
There were two minor goals that I had for this game: I wanted to ride 
a dragon and I wanted to try for wishless.  The first step was getting 
a spellbook of polymorph.  So, with 2 wands of polymorph, I blessed 
and polymorphed all of my spellbooks, read the new ones, and 
polymorphed again.  Eventually I had an almost complete repertoire and 
with maxed out luck it did not take too long to get a Book of 
Polymorph.  From there it only took another 30,000+ turns of 
collecting tools and polypiling before I had a saddle.  I was really 
surprised that it took so long.  I don't think there was a single pick-
axe, mirror, or key left in the dungeon! That saddle was eventually 
disintegrated before I had the opportunity to go against people's 
advice and ride the dragon through the Endgame.

Breaking Wishless:
I had originally planned on this game being wishless and thus I began 
collecting magical armor for use in polypiling.  I had already 
acquired almost all of my kit from found treasure including a set of 
homemade silver dragon scales from the castle but I still needed a 
cloak of magic resistance.  After days of playing went by collecting 
every piece of scrap armor in the dungeon and polypiling, I eventually 
wished for my cloak.  I probably spent an extra 20,000 turns 
polypiling armor trying to make a cloak.  I did not want my only 
source of MR to be my quest artifact.  Oh well, I'll work on conducts 
once I have some skills.  The other mistake I made was not blessing 
all of the armor that I was polypiling.  Sure, I blessed wands and 
amulets but I figured that the sheer numbers of elven cloaks and boots 
that were being zapped would eventually yield a cloak of MR. In 
retrospect, given how many extra potions I had remaining, I should 
have dipped the armor to reduce the chance of shuddering.

Final Preparation:
By this time, my character was pretty powerful with heaps of 
alchemised full healing and it was a question of what to bring with me 
on the run.  Having never ascended before, I did not know quite what 
to bring, but against everyone's advice I stayed burdened until I 
arrived on the Astral Plane.  My reasoning was that I knew what I was 
going to drop if necessary and most of my belongings were being used 
up on the run (potions of paralysis, gain level, wands of teleport).

Sadly I lost my favorite pet to a polymorph trap in the lower levels 
of Gehennom.  Suddenly I saw a mumak dropping a +6 rustproof dwarvish 
mattock that had belonged to my level 28 Titan.  That was a great pet 
and I had hoped to bring him with me until the end but alas, I got 
careless.  He was either my second or third pet so I had him for over 
150,000 turns!

I left all of my trophies at my stash and brought only what I thought 
necessary including a magic marker and blank scrolls, which turned out 
useful for the plane of water.  I also saved one blessed scroll of 
genocide that was used to genocide ;'s once I arrived on the PoW.  My 
run strategy was to levelport from the wizard's tower to the VS, then 
into the left side of the sanctum, quick grab the amulet and c!GL up.  
Then with teleport control, I would zap myself to the upstairs on my 
way up through hell.

To deal with the Wizard I had many wands of death and a stack of 
paralysis potions.  I would wield the potion, hit him, and just carry 
on (with the benefit of a =oFA).

Some statistics:
Traversing from level 48 to the VotD on 28 (20 levels) took me 37 
climbs.  It sure felt like a lot, especially when I ran out of c!oGL 
for the no-teleport levels.  I forgot to note the turn when I killed 
the wizard but it was probably 164437 and I climbed the upstairs from 
level 1 on turn 165321, so it took me about 900 turns.  Fast or slow? 
I'm not sure, but I don't think that I could have sped it up any.  
During the run I zapped or ^T a total of 64 times and 34 were 
successful, which seems a bit unlucky at 53.1%.  I suffered a 
malignant aura once, and Rodney found me only twice, both times in the 
main dungeon, and both times I hit him with a potion of paralysis and 
kept running.  I was surprised, however that when I arrived on the 
plane of earth to not see him (as I've posted in a separate thread).  
I suppose he was still paralyzed.

The Astral plane was straightforward.  I had not taken my ring of 
conflict off upon arrival so the two hostile angels required 
teleporting.  Then I dropped enough wands and scrolls to get to 
unburdened and melee'd Death.  With 500+ hp he is not so dangerous.  
Then some teleport zaps later I walked right over and the first altar 
was lawful.

As you can see below, I was way overprepared in terms of remaining 
zaps, hp and pw.  But, it was my first time and I wanted to make sure 
I did not end up somewhere missing something important.  Also, I 
enjoyed playing around in the game and by the time I got over 100k 
turns I had accumulated so many potions of foo-healing that the hp 
gain from alchemy alone was probably 400+ hp.

